WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.810
You say you don't like a song, and the person across from you says "there is no arguing about taste," and the matter is closed.

2
00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:16.260
But if that were really so, no one would recommend a film to anyone, no dish would count as "genuinely good," no building could be ugly.

3
00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:21.990
So if beauty belongs entirely to you, why do you want everyone to like what you like?

4
00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:33.840
A friend holds out one of their earbuds to you.

5
00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:41.810
There is a strange tension in their face; from the moment the song begins, they are not watching you so much as watching your expression.

6
00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:48.340
Forty seconds later, when you take the earbud out and say "not bad," something in that face gives way.

7
00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:50.870
A small but genuine wound.

8
00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:56.440
And yet nothing insulting has happened, there is no quarrel, no clash of interests.

9
00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:01.370
There is only a song, and two people who did not feel the same thing about it.

10
00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:04.060
So why did it sting so much?

11
00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:08.510
Because when they held out that earbud, they were not really handing you a song.

12
00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:10.880
They were handing you a claim.

13
00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:15.810
They were saying "this is beautiful," and taking for granted that you would see the same thing.

14
00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:20.260
What comes to the surface when you don't is not a difference of taste.

15
00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:22.950
It is a refused invitation.

16
00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:27.720
There is a saying everyone knows: there is no disputing tastes and colors.

17
00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:35.210
Translated from a Latin phrase, repeated for centuries, laid on the table so that arguments at dinner do not get out of hand.

18
00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:41.580
But notice this: the people who say that sentence are arguing again five minutes after they have said it.

19
00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:52.910
About which series had a disastrous final season, which singer was never really any good, what music will be played at the wedding, what color the wall of the house should be, who ordered correctly at the restaurant.

20
00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:57.360
It may be the most frequently violated principle in human history.

21
00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:02.130
We say taste cannot be disputed, and we dispute it without stopping.

22
00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:05.300
Which means the sentence is not an observation.

23
00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:07.990
It is an offer of ceasefire.

24
00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:10.520
And it does not hold.

25
00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:15.610
The reason it does not hold is hidden in a simple confusion of language.

26
00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:20.860
We say two separate things, and most of the time we do not notice the difference between them.

27
00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:30.910
One is "I like it." The other is "it is beautiful." These are not two phrasings of the same sentence; they are two different claims.

28
00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:35.200
When I say "I love this soup," I am demanding nothing from anyone.

29
00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:45.410
If you do not love it, there is no problem; in fact, when you tell me you do not, I do not withdraw my claim, because the claim was confined to my own palate from the start.

30
00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:50.020
But when I say "this view is beautiful," the situation changes.

31
00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:53.990
When you shrug and walk on, an objection rises inside me.

32
00:02:53.990 --> 00:02:56.840
As if you had missed something.

33
00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:02.410
As if there were something standing there that both of us were supposed to see, and you did not look.

34
00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:11.980
And the whole matter lies in that word "supposed." The judgment of taste, the most private-seeming judgment we make, carries a hidden "everyone" inside it.

35
00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:23.150
One of the first people to lay this out openly was David Hume, who in 1757 wrote an essay called "Of the Standard of Taste." Hume was standing in the middle of a strange contradiction.

36
00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:32.400
On one side, he held that beauty lies not in objects but in the mind that beholds them; every mind sees a different beauty, and no one is in error.

37
00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:44.050
On the other side, there was something everyone knew: nobody seriously agrees with a person who sets an ordinary poem next to Homer and says "these two are equally good." Such a person's taste is not different.

38
00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:45.940
It is faulty.

39
00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:50.870
To untie this knot, Hume borrowed a scene from Don Quixote.

40
00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:55.480
Sancho tells of two of his kinsmen who had a reputation as judges of wine.

41
00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:58.650
A cask is to be tasted.

42
00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:12.060
The first one tastes it, thinks for a moment, and says, "good, but there is a faint taste of iron in it." The second tastes it and says, "good, but I get a taste of leather." The people around them laugh,

43
00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:14.110
and the two men are mocked.

44
00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:21.120
When the cask is drained to the bottom, a small key is found there, and attached to the key, an old leather thong.

45
00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:28.450
The conclusion Hume drew from this is that the palate is not an organ of opinion but an organ of perception, and it grows sharper.

46
00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:32.420
What those two men felt was not imagined; it was there.

47
00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:36.550
It simply lay below the threshold of perception of most people.

48
00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:41.160
The same holds for painting, for music, for the novel.

49
00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:48.650
This is why Hume argued that taste could have a standard, but he looked for that standard not in the object, in a long labor instead.

50
00:04:48.650 --> 00:05:01.900
A good judge, in his account, had five qualities: a refined sensibility, the practice that comes from working through a great many works, the habit of comparison, the ability to shake off prejudice, and good sense.

51
00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:08.590
People who carry all five at once are few, but they exist, and in time their shared verdict emerges.

52
00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:14.320
Hume was an honest man; he also wrote that the standard he defended did not settle everything.

53
00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:23.250
There is nothing faulty in a twenty-year-old loving love poems while a sixty-year-old loves sober reflection; differences of temperament and age are permanent.

54
00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:28.980
So what we have is neither complete arbitrariness nor a measuring stick binding on everyone.

55
00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:32.790
But Hume's answer leaves the real question open.

56
00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:36.600
Very well, some people's palates may be keener.

57
00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:44.090
Yet why is it that I, never once certain of the keenness of my own palate, still want my friend to love that song when I play it for them?

58
00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:48.860
Why do I not simply say "that is how my palate is" and fall silent?

59
00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:51.550
Where does this insistence come from?

60
00:05:51.550 --> 00:06:00.160
The man who asked this question in history's most uncomfortable form was a philosopher who barely traveled anywhere in his whole life, in Königsberg,

61
00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:05.570
and whose daily walk was said to be so punctual that his neighbors set their clocks by it.

62
00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:15.780
In the Critique of the Power of Judgment, published in 1790, Immanuel Kant went inside the judgment of taste and found there something that looks logically impossible.

63
00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:20.390
Kant begins with a distinction, and that distinction carries everything.

64
00:06:20.390 --> 00:06:23.880
He says there are three ways we take pleasure in a thing.

65
00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:30.090
The first is the agreeable: the pleasure of the palate, of the skin, of the appetite.

66
00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:36.460
The second is the good: what we approve of because it works, because it is right, because it is useful.

67
00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:41.070
The third is the beautiful, and it resembles neither of the other two.

68
00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:44.240
To show the difference he gives his own example.

69
00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:56.690
A man says, "Canary wine is agreeable." When someone contradicts him, he corrects himself at once: "agreeable to me." Nobody is offended; everyone knows that the pleasure of the tongue is personal,

70
00:06:56.690 --> 00:07:06.100
and that the natural home of such a sentence is "to me." Then Kant says the striking thing: when it comes to the beautiful, that line of retreat is closed.

71
00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:10.230
The sentence "this building is beautiful to me" is ridiculous.

72
00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:17.240
The person who claims beauty refuses from the outset to say "to me," and makes the claim on everyone's behalf.

73
00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:19.770
Where does that right come from?

74
00:07:19.770 --> 00:07:23.100
Kant's answer still gives a person pause today.

75
00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:26.750
The judgment of beauty, he says, is disinterested.

76
00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:33.280
That is, I take pleasure in the thing without wanting to own it, to consume it, to draw any advantage from it.

77
00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:39.330
A hungry person's pleasure in bread is interested; they want the bread to exist and to be in their hand.

78
00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:45.700
But the person standing before a sunset, before the pattern on a tile, before a melody, wants nothing.

79
00:07:45.700 --> 00:07:48.870
They only look, and looking is enough.

80
00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:54.280
And this disinterestedness, for Kant, is the source of the claim to universality.

81
00:07:54.280 --> 00:08:02.890
Think of it this way: if my pleasure does not come from my appetite, my need, my interest, then there is nothing peculiar to me in that pleasure.

82
00:08:02.890 --> 00:08:08.300
If my stomach is not in play, what remains must be something shared among human beings.

83
00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:18.190
And at exactly this point my mind quietly makes a leap: since this pleasure did not arise in me from any private cause, it must arise in everyone who looks at the same thing.

84
00:08:18.190 --> 00:08:21.200
I do not do this deliberately.

85
00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:26.770
By the moment the sentence "this is beautiful" leaves my mouth, the leap has already happened.

86
00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:32.660
Kant calls this subjective universality, and he is aware of how strange the term is.

87
00:08:32.660 --> 00:08:40.950
The judgment is subjective, because what it rests on is not a proof, a measurement or a concept, but a state that wakes up inside me.

88
00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:45.880
Yet the same judgment is universal, because it demands that everyone agree.

89
00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:51.930
The word Kant chooses here is a very careful one: it demands, but it cannot compel.

90
00:08:51.930 --> 00:09:01.020
The judgment of taste begs the other person for assent, waits for assent, believes it deserves assent, and has not a single argument with which to prove it.

91
00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:05.790
Ask someone who finds a painting beautiful, "why?" They will tell you.

92
00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:09.760
They will speak of the colors, the composition, the light.

93
00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:21.090
But all of it is not proof; at most it is a description, a pointing, an elaborate way of saying "look over there." The other person can accept every word of it and still say, "even so,

94
00:09:21.090 --> 00:09:26.500
I do not find it beautiful," and logically they will have made no mistake.

95
00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:31.590
Kant writes it plainly: nobody can make me find something beautiful by proof.

96
00:09:31.590 --> 00:09:36.520
A hundred people may praise a poem, and I can shake my head and walk on.

97
00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:38.570
And still we insist.

98
00:09:38.570 --> 00:09:41.420
This is the real door Kant opens.

99
00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:50.670
What makes us insist is not arrogance but a strange promise a person makes to themselves: if the eyes that look are shared, then what is seen must be shared too.

100
00:09:50.670 --> 00:09:58.160
Kant calls this the common sense, that shared sensitivity we assume everyone possesses and can never prove.

101
00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:02.770
The judgment of taste is a wager that this common sense exists.

102
00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:09.140
Every "this is beautiful" really means: I believe I am living in the same world as you.

103
00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:13.110
That is why the friend handing the earbud back is wounded.

104
00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:16.120
What was refused is not a song.

105
00:10:16.120 --> 00:10:18.170
It is that wager.

106
00:10:18.170 --> 00:10:22.460
And that is why quarrels about taste can be so poisonous.

107
00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:29.790
We have no proof, and we cannot give up the claim either; when those two come together, a person raises their voice.

108
00:10:29.790 --> 00:10:38.560
Families who stop speaking for two days over the song to be played at a wedding, couples who return for years to the same argument about the color of the house,

109
00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:44.930
people who quietly respect a friend a little less for not liking a film, are all inside the same deadlock.

110
00:10:44.930 --> 00:10:49.220
Kant does not resolve this deadlock.

111
00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:50.950
He names it.

112
00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:55.720
Taste cannot be argued, because it does not move by concepts.

113
00:10:55.720 --> 00:11:00.810
But taste is contested, because everyone believes the other person could see it too.

114
00:11:00.810 --> 00:11:03.340
Kant stops here.

115
00:11:03.340 --> 00:11:07.790
He does not tell us whether there is a way to be taught how to look.

116
00:11:07.790 --> 00:11:14.320
And yet, centuries earlier, an entirely different tradition had already begun searching for the answer.

117
00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:22.450
In Islamic thought, beauty is not an unarguable private affair but, on the contrary, one of the most widely discussed of subjects.

118
00:11:22.450 --> 00:11:28.020
The starting point is usually the same tradition: God is beautiful and loves beauty.

119
00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:35.670
The ground this sentence opens up is broad, because beauty here is not an ornament but a quality of being itself.

120
00:11:35.670 --> 00:11:43.320
The word jamal is used precisely to convey this: a beauty beyond the visible, overflowing from existence itself.

121
00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:47.770
One of those who took the most striking step within this frame is al-Ghazali.

122
00:11:47.770 --> 00:11:58.620
In the section on love and affection in his great work The Revival of the Religious Sciences, he asks why the human being loves what is beautiful, and he begins by correcting a common error.

123
00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:06.270
Beauty, he says, is imagined to be something the eye alone sees; but the eye captures only a part of beauty.

124
00:12:06.270 --> 00:12:12.000
The eye sees the beauty of a piece of writing, but it cannot see the beauty of a moral character.

125
00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:21.090
There is a beauty in a just person, in a generous act, in a steadfast friendship, and what perceives it is not the work of the eye but of inner sight, of insight.

126
00:12:21.090 --> 00:12:27.940
Then he offers a criterion that is still unsettling today: the beauty of anything lies in its own perfection.

127
00:12:27.940 --> 00:12:32.390
A thing is beautiful to the degree that it reaches the completeness possible for it.

128
00:12:32.390 --> 00:12:40.840
The beauty of a horse and the beauty of a piece of calligraphy are not the same thing; what makes the horse beautiful will not make the writing beautiful.

129
00:12:40.840 --> 00:12:47.050
Whatever is expected of a horse, the flawless fulfillment of that expectation is the horse's beauty.

130
00:12:47.050 --> 00:12:53.260
This is an idea that rescues beauty from arbitrariness without locking it inside a single mold.

131
00:12:53.260 --> 00:12:58.190
Beauty is in the object, but in each object according to its own nature.

132
00:12:58.190 --> 00:13:02.640
Avicenna enters the question from somewhere else.

133
00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:14.130
In his treatise on love, he says that everything in existence carries an inclination toward its own perfection; the stone tends toward its stoniness, the plant toward its plantness, the human being toward humanity.

134
00:13:14.130 --> 00:13:19.700
For him this tending is a kind of love, and at the far end of love stands beauty.

135
00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:29.590
That causeless pull a person feels before something beautiful is, in Avicenna's eyes, no whim, but the echo in the human being of the most fundamental movement of existence.

136
00:13:29.590 --> 00:13:33.880
Now put these two ideas side by side and see what follows.

137
00:13:33.880 --> 00:13:42.010
If beauty is a form of completeness in the object, and if what perceives it is an inner sense that can be trained, then taste is not a preference.

138
00:13:42.010 --> 00:13:43.900
It is an ability.

139
00:13:43.900 --> 00:13:46.270
And abilities are learned.

140
00:13:46.270 --> 00:13:50.560
This is where the weight the word zevk carries in this tradition comes from.

141
00:13:50.560 --> 00:13:58.210
Zevk means, at root, to taste: not to describe a thing from outside, but to have tasted it directly.

142
00:13:58.210 --> 00:14:05.220
A saying often repeated among the Sufis puts it this way: the one who has not tasted does not know.

143
00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:12.230
This is not a sentence that shuts the door on knowledge; it says that one kind of knowledge can only be acquired by living it.

144
00:14:12.230 --> 00:14:17.320
A person who has memorized a book on swimming does not know how to swim.

145
00:14:17.320 --> 00:14:24.810
In the same way, a person who has read an essay explaining why a composition is beautiful has not yet heard that composition.

146
00:14:24.810 --> 00:14:30.860
This is why, in the Ottoman world, art was transmitted not through textbooks but through meşk.

147
00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:38.830
In Turkish classical music, meşk means learning a piece in the presence of a master, without notation, by singing it over and over.

148
00:14:38.830 --> 00:14:41.840
It takes years.

149
00:14:41.840 --> 00:14:49.010
What you learn is not only the melody; it is where a note should bend, where it should be held, where it should not be touched at all.

150
00:14:49.010 --> 00:14:51.860
In other words, the ear is trained.

151
00:14:51.860 --> 00:15:03.670
Calligraphy has its own version: the student writes the same letter thousands of times, the master takes the pen and strikes a line through it, and one day the student looks at a letter of their own and can say,

152
00:15:03.670 --> 00:15:10.040
for the first time, "that has not come out right." The moment they can say it is the moment their eye has opened.

153
00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:12.570
Nobody showed them a proof.

154
00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:14.940
Somebody taught them how to look.

155
00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:20.350
Now we can return to our opening question, and the answer suddenly becomes much clearer.

156
00:15:20.350 --> 00:15:28.480
What lies beneath the wish that everyone should love what you love is, most of the time, neither possessiveness nor a sense of superiority.

157
00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:32.770
It is the conviction: I can see this, and you can see it too.

158
00:15:32.770 --> 00:15:39.780
Kant named that conviction and could not prove it; this tradition, instead of proving it, turned it into a path.

159
00:15:39.780 --> 00:15:45.990
If you cannot see it, you are not defective; it means you have not looked yet, and looking can be learned.

160
00:15:45.990 --> 00:15:49.640
But right here the shadow of the same idea appears.

161
00:15:49.640 --> 00:15:57.770
Someone who says that seeing beauty can be learned slides very easily into another sentence: if you cannot see what I see, you are behind.

162
00:15:57.770 --> 00:16:03.980
The idea of a trainable sense can turn, in the hand of the one doing the training, into a rod.

163
00:16:03.980 --> 00:16:10.830
And the brake inside this tradition is in the same place: beauty invites, it does not compel.

164
00:16:10.830 --> 00:16:17.840
Nobody can force meşk on another; a student made to sit down by force will write the letter but will not see it.

165
00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:24.210
That is the fine line between invitation and imposition, and holding that line is harder than seeing beauty.

166
00:16:24.210 --> 00:16:30.420
Now we come to a point where this entire discussion is changing seriously for the first time in 2 centuries.

167
00:16:30.420 --> 00:16:34.230
Because we no longer hold our tastes out to one another.

168
00:16:34.230 --> 00:16:36.600
We record them somewhere.

169
00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:44.090
In February 2009, Facebook opened up what is now the most familiar symbol on the internet, the like button.

170
00:16:44.090 --> 00:16:51.580
The idea was innocent: people should not have to write a comment on everything; they should be able to give their approval with a single tap.

171
00:16:51.580 --> 00:16:55.230
But with that single tap came a silent transformation.

172
00:16:55.230 --> 00:17:02.080
Until that day, a liking was a sentence said to somebody; now it became a piece of data given to a system.

173
00:17:02.080 --> 00:17:03.970
The difference looks small.

174
00:17:03.970 --> 00:17:06.340
It is not.

175
00:17:06.340 --> 00:17:12.230
When you say "this song is beautiful," the one you are addressing is a human being who can contradict you.

176
00:17:12.230 --> 00:17:20.840
When you press the like button, the one you are addressing is a model, and it does not contradict; it does not try to understand you, it predicts you.

177
00:17:20.840 --> 00:17:26.410
What Kant called the judgment that demands everyone's assent turns into the input of a prediction engine.

178
00:17:26.410 --> 00:17:31.820
You no longer need to persuade anyone, because the system is not asking you for a reason.

179
00:17:31.820 --> 00:17:34.190
It is only asking for a signal.

180
00:17:34.190 --> 00:17:37.520
The scale of this is larger than people imagine.

181
00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:48.050
According to a paper published by Netflix's own researchers in 2015, roughly 80 percent of the hours watched on the platform came from its recommendation systems, the rest from search.

182
00:17:48.050 --> 00:17:56.820
YouTube's product manager Neal Mohan said in 2018 that around 70 percent of watch time on the platform came from the recommendation algorithm.

183
00:17:56.820 --> 00:18:04.150
So the great majority of what now comes before us is neither something we went looking for nor something a friend held out to us.

184
00:18:04.150 --> 00:18:06.680
It is brought toward us.

185
00:18:06.680 --> 00:18:18.810
Eli Pariser, in his book The Filter Bubble, published in 2011, discussed the political consequences of this arrangement; he described how everyone began living inside a feed that confirms their own view.

186
00:18:18.810 --> 00:18:25.340
But the side of the matter that faces taste is quieter than its political side, and perhaps more lasting.

187
00:18:25.340 --> 00:18:28.670
Because what is lost here is not accurate information.

188
00:18:28.670 --> 00:18:31.520
It is the shared object.

189
00:18:31.520 --> 00:18:33.730
Think of it this way.

190
00:18:33.730 --> 00:18:38.500
All that contention Kant described rests on two people looking at the same thing.

191
00:18:38.500 --> 00:18:43.110
The same painting, the same film, the same folk song.

192
00:18:43.110 --> 00:18:51.560
Without a shared object there is nothing about which one can say "you see it too." And recommendation systems dissolve exactly the shared object.

193
00:18:51.560 --> 00:19:00.170
Everyone is given their own feed, their own home page, their own cover image; even the poster for the same series is shown differently to two people.

194
00:19:00.170 --> 00:19:11.340
To return to Kant's own example, the system serves everyone their own Canary wine, and nobody has to say "to me" anymore, because they are no longer drinking from the same glass as anyone.

195
00:19:11.340 --> 00:19:14.830
The second consequence is more troubling still.

196
00:19:14.830 --> 00:19:18.480
A recommendation system is built to predict what you will like.

197
00:19:18.480 --> 00:19:22.450
Which means it takes you as you are and serves you as you are.

198
00:19:22.450 --> 00:19:31.700
The training described by al-Ghazali, or by the meşk tradition, works in exactly the opposite direction: it sits you down in front of something you do not like today,

199
00:19:31.700 --> 00:19:36.790
something you may even find repellent on first hearing, and asks you to stay there.

200
00:19:36.790 --> 00:19:44.600
A work that seems dull on the first listening can open up on the third, but only if there is persistence between the first and the third.

201
00:19:44.600 --> 00:19:52.410
The algorithm has no such persistence; it sees that you gave up in the first 10 seconds, and it never knocks on that door again.

202
00:19:52.410 --> 00:19:55.580
It knows you, but it does not teach you how to look.

203
00:19:55.580 --> 00:20:01.150
And so that old distinction between "I like it" and "it is beautiful" is slowly being erased.

204
00:20:01.150 --> 00:20:10.400
The system can measure only the first, because that is the only measurable thing: did you click, how many seconds did you stay, did you watch it to the end.

205
00:20:10.400 --> 00:20:15.490
The second, a thing defended on everyone's behalf, enters into no measurement.

206
00:20:15.490 --> 00:20:19.300
And what is not measured comes in time to be counted as unimportant.

207
00:20:19.300 --> 00:20:24.230
That is why insisting today carries a different meaning than it did 30 years ago.

208
00:20:24.230 --> 00:20:34.280
When you hand a friend a book, when you take a nephew or a niece to their first concert, when you tell somebody "watch this with me," what you are really doing is resisting being predicted.

209
00:20:34.280 --> 00:20:39.370
You are building with your own hands a shared object that does not otherwise exist.

210
00:20:39.370 --> 00:20:44.300
But this resistance can easily turn into something else, and it does.

211
00:20:44.300 --> 00:20:55.310
We have all seen what arguments about taste look like on the internet: humiliating the person who loves what you do not love, turning taste into a badge of identity, saying with perfect ease,

212
00:20:55.310 --> 00:21:01.200
"nothing good ever came of anyone who likes that." This is the diseased form of insistence.

213
00:21:01.200 --> 00:21:09.330
To see the difference it is enough to return to Kant's careful word: the judgment of taste demands assent, it cannot extract it.

214
00:21:09.330 --> 00:21:16.020
The person who knows they hold no proof invites; the person who imagines they hold proof passes sentence.

215
00:21:16.020 --> 00:21:20.790
Perhaps what this really leaves us with is that the question itself has turned around.

216
00:21:20.790 --> 00:21:26.520
When we asked why I want everyone to love what I love, we assumed the answer was selfishness.

217
00:21:26.520 --> 00:21:28.250
It was not.

218
00:21:28.250 --> 00:21:36.700
That desire turns out to be a refusal to close in on oneself, a way of seeing the person across from you as someone capable of looking at the same world you do.

219
00:21:36.700 --> 00:21:40.510
Nobody holds out an earbud to a person they do not care about.

220
00:21:40.510 --> 00:21:45.120
So the real question becomes this: how are we to carry that desire.

221
00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:50.050
We have no proof, and we never will; and staying silent is beyond us.

222
00:21:50.050 --> 00:21:52.900
One honest posture is left.

223
00:21:52.900 --> 00:22:01.190
To sit down beside someone, press play, and stay there, knowing full well that you cannot force anyone to find a thing beautiful.

224
00:22:01.190 --> 00:22:05.160
To be hurt if they do not like it, and still not give up.

225
00:22:05.160 --> 00:22:09.930
Because that hurt is the firmest proof that we still believe in a world held in common.

226
00:22:09.930 --> 00:22:13.580
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

227
00:22:13.580 --> 00:22:17.390
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
